  "worked_solution": "Since $x$ represents the number of tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily, and since the company produces at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<1\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-0.004x+3.9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -0.004x+3.9 & =0 \\\\ x & =975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=975$ lies in the interval $0<x<1\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=975$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.004<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=975$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=975$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $975\\cdot 10=9\\,750$ kilograms of coffee per day to maximize its daily profit.",
